body:
**Describe the bug**

Consider

```
#include <cudf/column/column_factories.hpp>
#include <cudf/io/parquet.hpp>
#include <cudf/io/types.hpp>
#include <cudf/scalar/scalar.hpp>
#include <cudf/types.hpp>
#include <limits>
#include <tuple>

int main() {
  constexpr size_t num_rows = (1L << 31) - 4999;

  static_assert(num_rows <= std::numeric_limits<cudf::size_type>::max(),
                "Too many rows");

  auto sink = cudf::io::sink_info("foo.pq");

  auto val = cudf::numeric_scalar<bool>(true);
  auto col = cudf::make_column_from_scalar(val, num_rows);
  auto options = cudf::io::parquet_writer_options::builder(
                     sink, cudf::table_view({col->view()}))
                     .build();

  std::ignore = cudf::io::write_parquet(options);
  return 0;
}
```

Which creates a boolean column of _nearly_ `size_type::max()` rows and then attempts to write it to a parquet file.

```
$ ./io-bug
[600839][11:34:44:775673][error ] Parquet writer encountered exception during processing. No data has been written to the sink.
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'rmm::out_of_memory'
  what():  std::bad_alloc: out_of_memory: CUDA error (failed to allocate 18446744073692371776 bytes) at: /home/coder/rmm/cpp/include/rmm/mr/device/cuda_memory_resource.hpp:62: cudaErrorMemoryAllocation out of memory
Aborted (core dumped)
```

This looks to be due to incorrect use of `div_rounding_up_unsafe` in the parquet writer.

When converting the table to parquet data we do:
```
  std::vector<int> num_frag_in_part;
  std::transform(partitions.begin(),
                 partitions.end(),
                 std::back_inserter(num_frag_in_part),
                 [max_page_fragment_size](auto const& part) {
                   return util::div_rounding_up_unsafe(part.num_rows, max_page_fragment_size);
                 });
```

In this case, `part.num_rows` is $2^{31} - 4999$ and `max_page_fragment_size` is $5000$. `div_rounding_up_unsafe` does `(dividend + divisor - 1) / divisor` which here is $(2^{31} - 4999 + 5000 - 1) / 5000$. But $2^{31} - 4999 + 5000$ overflows the int32 limit and we end up with `num_frag_in_part == -429497` and then when allocate the row_group_fragments we get nonsense.

A minimal fix appears to be:

```diff
diff --git a/cpp/src/io/parquet/writer_impl.cu b/cpp/src/io/parquet/writer_impl.cu
index 621eadb0e5..342f7aeefb 100644
--- a/cpp/src/io/parquet/writer_impl.cu
+++ b/cpp/src/io/parquet/writer_impl.cu
@@ -1766,7 +1766,7 @@ auto convert_table_to_parquet_data(table_input_metadata& table_meta,
                  partitions.end(),
                  std::back_inserter(num_frag_in_part),
                  [max_page_fragment_size](auto const& part) {
-                   return util::div_rounding_up_unsafe(part.num_rows, max_page_fragment_size);
+                   return util::div_rounding_up_safe<int>(part.num_rows, max_page_fragment_size);
                  });

   size_type num_fragments = std::reduce(num_frag_in_part.begin(), num_frag_in_part.end());
```

But there are a number of other div_rounding_up_unsafe calls in this file which I think should also be audited.

Running the following test (data file attached), which is compiled using CUDA 11:
```
#include <cudf_test/base_fixture.hpp>
#include <cudf_test/column_wrapper.hpp>

#include <cudf/io/parquet.hpp>
#include <cudf/join.hpp>

struct Test : public cudf::test::BaseFixture {};

TEST_F(Test, XXX)
{
  std::string f1 = "f1.parquet";
  std::string f2 = "f2.parquet";

  cudf::io::parquet_reader_options opts1 =
    cudf::io::parquet_reader_options::builder(cudf::io::source_info{f1});
  cudf::io::parquet_reader_options opts2 =
    cudf::io::parquet_reader_options::builder(cudf::io::source_info{f2});

  auto out1 = cudf::io::read_parquet(opts1);
  auto out2 = cudf::io::read_parquet(opts2);
  auto t1   = std::move(out1.tbl);
  auto t2   = std::move(out2.tbl);

  {
    auto const [left_indices, right_indices] =
      cudf::inner_join(t1->view(), t2->view(), cudf::null_equality::EQUAL);
    printf("size: %zu | %zu\n", left_indices->size(), right_indices->size());
  }

  {
    double const load_factor = 0.5;
    cudf::distinct_hash_join hash(t2->view(), cudf::null_equality::EQUAL, load_factor);
    auto const [left_indices, right_indices] = hash.inner_join(t1->view());

    printf("size: %zu | %zu\n", left_indices->size(), right_indices->size());
  }
}
```
Output of the first join is always consistent:
```
size: 100000 | 100000
```

The second join produces different outputs in each run. For example:
```
size: 99693 | 99693
...
size: 99603 | 99603
...
size: 99622 | 99622
```

Note that this issue does not show up when building with CUDA 12.

[data.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/19948244/data.zip)

In the device code in `rolling_detail.cuh`, there are variables declared as `volatile` to work around compiler optimizations in CUDA 10.x. E.g.:
```c++
    // declare this as volatile to avoid some compiler optimizations that lead to incorrect results
    // for CUDA 10.0 and below (fixed in CUDA 10.1)
    volatile cudf::size_type count = 0;
```
Given that CUDF 0.20 supports CUDA 11.0+, it should now be safe to remove this comment, and the `volatile` attributes.

If not handled in #8158, this can be addressed after #8158 is merged.
